      "content": "# Transaction Isolation, Race Conditions, and Deadlock Prevention\n\n## 1. Transaction Isolation\n\nTransaction isolation defines how and when changes made by one transaction become visible to other concurrent transactions. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, each preventing specific anomalies:\n\n### Isolation Levels (from lowest to highest)\n\n| Level | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read |\n|-------|-----------|---------------------|--------------|\n| Read Uncommitted | Possible | Possible | Possible |\n| Read Committed | Prevented | Possible | Possible |\n| Repeatable Read | Prevented | Prevented | Possible* |\n| Serializable | Prevented | Prevented | Prevented |\n\n*\\*Some databases (e.g., PostgreSQL) prevent phantom reads at Repeatable Read via MVCC.*\n\n### Example: Dirty Read\n\n```sql\n-- Transaction A\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\n-- Transaction A has NOT committed yet\n\n-- Transaction B (Read Uncommitted)\nBEGIN;\nSELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;\n-- B sees the uncommitted balance (e.g., 900 instead of 1000)\n--",
